==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|congregatus}}, past participle of {{m|la|congregare||to congregate}}, from {{m|la|con-||with, together}} + {{m|la|gregare||to collect into a flock}}, from {{m|la|grex||flock, herd}}. See [[gregarious]].

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˈkɒŋ.ɡɹə.ɡeɪt/|lang=en}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# {{lb|en|rare}} [[collective|Collective]]; [[assembled]]; [[compact]].
#* '''1605''', {{w|Francis Bacon}}, ''The Advancement of Learning'', Book II, Chapter IX:
#*: it means congratulations

===Verb===
{{en-verb|congregat}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To collect into an [[assembly]] or [[assemblage]]; to bring into one place, or into a united body
#: {{syn|en|assemble|gather together|mass|compact|bring together|Thesaurus:round up}}
#* {{rfdate|en|and other bibliographic particulars, eg, title of work, page, url, full name of author}} {{w|Hooker}}
#*: Any multitude of Christian men '''congregated''' may be termed by the name of a church.
#* {{rfdate|en|and other bibliographic particulars, eg, title of work, page, url}} {{w|Samuel Taylor Coleridge}}
#*: Cold '''congregates''' all bodies.
#* {{rfdate|en|and other bibliographic particulars, eg, title of work, page, url}} {{w|John Milton}}
#*: The great receptacle Of '''congregated''' waters he called Seas.
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To come together; to assemble; to [[meet]].
#: {{syn|en|assemble|begather|forgather|Thesaurus:assemble}}
#* {{rfdate|en|and other bibliographic particulars, eg, title of work, page, url, act, scene, line}} {{w|William Shakespeare}}
#*: Even there where merchants most do '''congregate'''.

